    "turn_idx": 53,
    "turn_text": "if I could end with that wall was meaningful. I, because I would stop at a place every day to make a point. So one of the places I went to was where they were learning how to work with, you know, artisans that were actually there that were working in plumbing and how to learn about the new plumbing tools and alliances, getting re-educated. You know, I'm a strong believer that's in my book that said we need training for a lifetime, because we put less into training and retraining our blue collar workers, our enlisted men and women that, as John Locke may have said, they're your greatest strength and they are still our greatest strength today in its civilian world than any other OECD developed nation. So I can understand why they sometimes sit there and think of a song by Pink called, What About Us? When they see all of a sudden college scholarships, but they want to know about us. Why isn't this a win-win where those tractors you saw out there in Iowa, as I talked to one person running back and forth in a parade said, hey, can you tell me, what do you do? And she said, I'm a digital agronomist. And I am going to make sure we don't need drivers of those tractors. Well, where's the program that really does say what about us? And that's what I mean by walk in other shoes. One other example, if you don't mind. Please. Each Veterans Day, even after left Congress until the epidemic, because I didn't want them to think I'd forgotten them to a penitentiary. Graded for Penitentiary was one I often went for, and I did the same in New Hampshire up there. When I went to the one in New Hampshire, I learned that this was one where they walk you in as a prisoner, and just like you walking into a dental office. You got two bar rooms here with bars, and then you could sit down there in a chair. And if you behave, they're gonna treat you as a respectful human being. And that's how the whole place was run. I just walked off through that maximum security prison. And I also did this and learned a lot about de-escalation, which, as you well know, is watching many videos recently, we have to understand as tough as it is, that is the right approach. And when you have something beginning untoward in violence, perhaps, rather than thinking you must take care of in a very aggressive way. And I learned that lesson there in that prison. But I also learned that these, when I went to the vets there in Gradyford, I was out there in with you. And I got a call if you don't mind. And it was great for prison on the line. And the inmate got on the line and said, because I used to go there each year and said, and he goes, hey, Joe, the guys have heard that you're running for president. They want you to know They're going to organize Philadelphia for you. Wow. What presidential candidate has that cocktail story, huh? But then my point was, again, service. Veterans never forget it. Back to your original point about bringing people together for national service. Yeah. Yeah, maybe I'm reaching a little bit here. But when you look at the recidivism programs, a lack thereof, And we have to keep in mind it was President Bush, who was the only president in America who ever mentioned recidivism in a state of the Union message, and then put $350 million into that program. And yet, we're still have about an 85% or so we sit at this rate in many of our programs. Bring all this up is you need to put yourself and other people's shoes and go there to really understand why they view things the way they do and try to correct the view if it's wrong, but if not, see if you can work together.",
